NAME
       pkg - package manager

SYNOPSIS
       **pkg** __operation__ [__options__] [__targets__]

DESCRIPTION
       **pkg** installs, removes and updates software packaged for PureC OS. It
       works like **pacman**, but there is no package database on a server: every
       package comes from its own GitHub repository listed as trusted in
       **/etc/pkg/pkg.conf**. A package is a release asset
       **name-version-arch.pkg.tar.gz**; the list of installed packages lives in
       **/var/lib/pkg/local**.

       Changing operations need root rights.

OPERATIONS
       **-S** __names__
              install packages and the packages they depend on. A name is a key of
              the **[trusted]** section or the GitHub address of a trusted repository.
              The package is downloaded first, its dependencies are read from it.
              **-Sy** asks GitHub for the newest release of every trusted repository,
              **-Su** upgrades every package that has a newer release, **-Syu** does both
              (like **pacman -Syu**): it shows the list and asks before changing anything.
       **-Ss** __text__   search the known releases
       **-Si** __name__   information about a package (full after it was downloaded)
       **-Sl**        list the trusted repositories and their newest versions
       **-Sc**        delete cached package files that are not installed
       **-R** __names__   remove packages. A package that another one needs is not removed
              (see **--nodeps**). **-Rs** also removes the dependencies that were
              installed only for them and nothing else needs.
       **-Q** [__names__]  list installed packages. **-Qi** information, **-Ql** files,
              **-Qo** __file__ the owner of a file, **-Qe** explicitly installed, **-Qd** installed
              as a dependency, **-Qs** __text__ search, **-Qu** packages with an update in the
              releases, **-Qk** check that the files exist, **-Qq** names only
       **-U** __files__   install package files from disk
       **-D** __names__   change the install reason: **--asdeps** or **--asexplicit**

OPTIONS
       **--root** __dir__    work on another root directory (databases, files and
                      the configuration are all taken under it)
       **--config** __file__ use another configuration file
       **--noconfirm**  do not ask questions
       **--needed**     do not reinstall a package that is up to date
       **--force**      overwrite files that exist but belong to no package
       **--nodeps**     do not check dependencies (**-Rd** is the same for removal)
       **--asdeps**, **--asexplicit**
                      set the install reason
       **-v**, **--version**

CONFIGURATION
       **/etc/pkg/pkg.conf** (the old place **/etc/pkg.conf** is read when it is
       missing) has an **[options]** section and a **[trusted]** section:

           [options]
           SigLevel = Optional

           [trusted]
           userspace = https://github.com/owner/userspace
           sdl2 = https://github.com/owner/SDL

       **Architecture** (x86_64) picks the asset, **Prerelease = yes** also takes
       prereleases, **API** changes the GitHub API address (a **file://** directory works
       for tests). A release is used when it has an asset named
       **name-version-arch.pkg.tar.gz** (or **-any**); among all releases the newest version
       wins. Packages are downloaded with **curl**.

       **SigLevel** is **Never** (no check), **Optional** (the asset **name.pkg.tar.gz.sig** is
       checked when the release has one, a bad one is always refused) or **Required**.
       The signature is ECDSA P-256 over SHA-512 of the package; the public keys are
       the files **/etc/pkg/keys/*.pub** (65 bytes, as hexadecimal text) until a keyring
       exists (see KEYRING). Without a signature a package is trusted because it comes from
       a trusted repository.

       **AllowLocalRepos = yes** enables the **[trusted]** repositories (shown as local).
       **AllowCommunity = yes** enables the **community** lines of **registry.conf** (default
       no, they are counted and reported as ignored). **KeyringThreshold = N** is the number
       of different master keys that must sign the keyring (default 1).

KEYRING
       Without **/etc/pkg/keyring.conf** every key in **/etc/pkg/keys/** is trusted for
       everything (legacy mode, pkg warns once that keys are not bound to repositories).
       With a keyring, a key is trusted only for what the keyring says. **keyring.conf**
       is text:

           serial 3
           key FP HEXPOINT official
           key FP HEXPOINT repo=https://github.com/owner/repo names=tool*,other
           revoke FP reason
           #sig MASTERFP HEXDER

       **FP** is the fingerprint of a key: the first 20 bytes of SHA-512 over its 65-byte
       point, in hexadecimal (40 characters; **pkgsign.py fingerprint**). The keyring is
       valid when at least **KeyringThreshold** different master keys (files
       **/etc/pkg/keys/master/*.pub**) made a **#sig** line, an ECDSA signature over every
       byte above the first **#sig** line. A keyring that is not valid is never used; if the
       installed one stops being valid nothing verifies.

       **pkg -Sy** downloads **keyring.conf** next to **registry.conf** and installs it only
       when it is valid and its **serial** is not lower than the installed one (a lower serial
       is refused as a rollback, the old keyring stays). Revoked keys are never accepted.

       Scopes: packages of official repositories, **registry.conf** and **repository.conf**
       must be signed by a key marked **official**. A package of a community repository
       must be signed by a key whose **repo** is that repository (case-insensitive,
       trailing slash and **.git** ignored) and whose **names** (fnmatch patterns on the
       package name) match the package. Packages of **[trusted]** repositories may also be
       signed by keys in **/etc/pkg/keys/local/*.pub**. Community packages always need a
       valid signature unless **SigLevel = Never**.

COMMUNITY REPOSITORIES
       **registry.conf** may contain lines **community https://github.com/owner/repo**.
       They are used only with **AllowCommunity = yes**. A community package whose name is
       the name of an official package (from official repositories or **repository.conf**)
       is dropped with a warning, so official names are reserved. Community packages are
       marked **community** in **-Ss**, **-Si** and **-Sl**, and a transaction that installs
       or upgrades them prints a warning first.

       The local database records the key that signed a package (**signer**) and its origin
       (official, community or local); **-Si** and **-Qi** show them. When the keyring
       revokes a key, pkg warns about installed packages signed by it, and **-Qu** lists
       them marked **REVOKED**.

PACKAGES
       Files listed in **backup** of a package are configuration files. When an upgrade
       finds such a file changed by you, your file stays and the new one is written next
       to it as **name.pkgnew**; when a package is removed a changed file is kept as
       **name.pkgsave**. A package can carry scripts that run through **/bin/sh** with
       **PKG_ROOT**, **PKG_NAME**, **PKG_VERSION** and **PKG_OLD_VERSION** set:
       **post-install**, **post-upgrade**, **pre-remove**, **post-remove**.

FILES
       /etc/pkg/pkg.conf, /etc/pkg/keys/, /etc/pkg/keys/master/, /etc/pkg/keys/local/, /etc/pkg/keyring.conf, /var/lib/pkg/local/, /var/lib/pkg/sync/,
       /var/cache/pkg/

SEE ALSO
       curl(1), sync(1)
